    "q-94030": {
      "dom_id": "step-q94030",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+13y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x),\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+13y=0,\\,y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-4.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "We find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP=2.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=-4,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3{e}^{-3x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+{e}^{-3x}(-2P\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x).\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=-4$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n-3P+2Q=-4.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $P=2$ gives $Q=1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x).\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
