    "q-130350": {
      "dom_id": "step-q130350",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,y(0)=4,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=8.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=4$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA=4.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=8,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=8$ into the above gives\n\n$$\nA+B=8.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $A=4$ gives $B=4.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=4{e}^{x}+4x{e}^{x}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
