    "q-130374": {
      "dom_id": "step-q130374",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+2y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x),\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+2y=0,\\,y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-2.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "We find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP=2.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=-2,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-{e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+{e}^{-x}(-P\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=-2$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n-P+Q=-2.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $P=2$ gives $Q=0.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
